{"name":"Krypton","hex":"#b8c0c3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Krypton","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A fictional planet, the native world of Superman, named after the element krypton."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Krypton"}],"entries":[{"title":"Krypton","blocks":[{"text":"Krypton is a chemical element; it has symbol Kr and atomic number 36. It is a colorless, odorless noble gas that occurs in trace amounts in the atmosphere and is often used with other rare gases in fluorescent lamps. Krypton is chemically inert."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Krypton_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Krypton_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krypton","wikidata":"Q888"},{"title":"Krypton (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Krypton is an American science fiction television series developed by David S. Goyer for Syfy. Focusing on Seg-El, the grandfather of DC Comics superhero Kal-El / Superman, the series is set approximately 200 years before the birth of Superman and takes place on the eponymous fictional planet. Krypton premiered on March 21, 2018. Its first season consists of ten episodes. The second season of ten episodes premiered on June 12, 2019. After its broadcast, Syfy canceled the series after two seasons in August."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c9/Krypton_%28TV_series%29_logo.png/330px-Krypton_%28TV_series%29_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krypton_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q24297601"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/William_Ramsay_working.jpg/500px-William_Ramsay_working.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/William_Ramsay_working.jpg/500px-William_Ramsay_working.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sir William Ramsay, the discoverer of krypton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Ramsay_working.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Krypton_hydride_crystal.jpg/330px-Krypton_hydride_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Krypton_hydride_crystal.jpg/330px-Krypton_hydride_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kr(H2)4 and H2 solids formed in a diamond anvil cell[33]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKrypton_hydride_crystal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Krypton_hydride_structure.png/500px-Krypton_hydride_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Krypton_hydride_structure.png/500px-Krypton_hydride_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of Kr(H2)4. Krypton octahedra (green) are surrounded by randomly oriented hydrogen molecules.[33]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKrypton_hydride_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/KrTube.jpg/500px-KrTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/KrTube.jpg/1280px-KrTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/KrTube.jpg/500px-KrTube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Krypton gas discharge tube","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKrTube.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikime